{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's groundbreaking drug decriminalization policy is set to expire, giving way to a new law that reclassifies possessing small amounts of hard drugs as a crime. Those caught can opt for either charges or treatment, involving behavioral health programs and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law, HB4002, aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and imposes harsher penalties for selling drugs near public spaces like parks. It incentivizes counties to establish treatment alternatives, diverting individuals from the justice system towards addiction and mental health support. Supporters argue this approach allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, while critics warn of potential inconsistencies and inequities in policy implementation.\n\n***\n\nEligibility for deflection is strictly defined, requiring no outstanding charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, as well as medical stability. If criteria are met, police will contact a county dispatch line to connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health team, which must make contact within 30 minutes. Failure to do so will result in arrest, as waiting is not an option.\n\n***\n\nPortland's Police Chief, Bob Day, highlighted the narrow deflection criteria and the strict 30-minute response window for behavioral health teams. This setup reflects the law's balance between enforcement and treatment options. The policy's effectiveness in addressing drug issues while providing adequate support will be closely monitored.\n\nP.P.S I recall a trip to another country where, regardless of age, people would eat local delicacies while shopping at markets, cleverly using their tongue to savor flavors.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.12320467829704285}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.284096896648407}, {"token": " groundbreaking", "logprob": -2.025684356689453}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.06386343389749527}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -4.351044481154531e-05}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": -2.47952248173533e-05}, {"token": " policy", "logprob": -0.35469767451286316}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.5763972997665405}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.022799616679549217}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": " expire", "logprob": -0.17750868201255798}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.002294528530910611}, {"token": " giving", "logprob": -0.24255236983299255}, {"token": " way", "logprob": -0.001503052655607462}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0022312526125460863}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -7.211902266135439e-05}, {"token": " law", "logprob": -0.00042572495294734836}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.28962060809135437}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -1.532196283340454}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.10726644098758698}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -8.702239938429557e-06}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -2.1478636264801025}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -3.4450891689630225e-05}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -0.012525111436843872}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.03659416735172272}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -9.894321920000948e-06}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.16219791769981384}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00010716341057559475}, {"token": " crime", "logprob": -0.0006055190460756421}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.014099156484007835}, {"token": " Those", "logprob": -0.4450489282608032}, {"token": " caught", "logprob": -0.045269183814525604}, {"token": " can", "logprob": -0.3041016161441803}, {"token": " opt", "log